---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { localePath } from "../../i18n";
import { GITHUB_URL } from "../../lib/site";
const lang = "en" as const;
---

<Base lang={lang} title="About Recompendium" description="What Recompendium is, which projects belong in the catalog, and how to propose one.">
  <main class="wrap detail">
    <div class="crumb"><a href={localePath(lang, "")}>← Catalog</a></div>
    <h1>About Recompendium</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A community-run catalog of console games running natively on PC: <b>static recompilations</b>,
      decompilations, ports, mods, and the tools that make them possible. None of it is emulation: the game
      ends up running on your machine as a native program.
    </p>

    <div class="sec-h"><h2>What’s in and what’s out</h2></div>
    <p class="about-copy">
      The catalog brings together five kinds of project: <b>static recompilations</b>
      (N64Recomp, XenonRecomp, ReXGlue…), <a href={localePath(lang, "ports")}>ports and remakes</a>
      built on a decompilation or made by fans (Ship of Harkinian, P-06…), the
      <a href={localePath(lang, "decompilaciones")}>decompilations</a> themselves,
      with source code rebuilt through reverse engineering, and the
      <a href={localePath(lang, "launchers")}>tools</a> that make it all possible, plus
      <a href={localePath(lang, "mods")}>mods</a>: mods for those games, editors for building content,
      and managers that install them.
    </p>
    <p class="about-copy">
      Emulators, FPGA cores, and any project that ships the game instead of asking for your own copy are
      left out. We cover those on the <a href={localePath(lang, "blog")}>blog</a>
      when they make the news, but they don’t join the catalog.
    </p>

    <div class="sec-h"><h2>Recomp vs. decompilation</h2></div>
    <p class="about-copy">
      A <b>recompilation</b> translates the game’s binary into native code: the result is already an
      executable for your PC. A <b>decompilation</b> takes a different route: it rebuilds readable source
      code, usually until it compiles back into the exact original binary. Once it matures, native ports
      grow out of it. That’s why we list both, but separately: a recomp already produces an executable;
      a decompilation at 30% doesn’t yet. Each decompilation’s percentage is updated daily from
      decomp.dev.
    </p>

    <div class="sec-h"><h2>A bit of history</h2></div>
    <p class="about-copy">
      The project that put the scene on the map was <b>Zelda 64: Recompiled</b> (Majora’s Mask), by
      Mr. Wiseguy, released in <b>May 2024</b> on top of N64Recomp and with its own built-in launcher.
      From there, everything grew fast: the Xbox 360 wave with XenonRecomp, the first standalone
      universal launcher (N64RecompLauncher, August 2025), Banjo-Kazooie (January 2026), and ReXGlue
      (February 2026).
    </p>

    <div class="sec-h"><h2>Want to add or fix something?</h2></div>
    <p class="about-copy">
      No coding needed. Use the
      <a href={localePath(lang, "contribuir")}>Contribute</a> form or the
      <b>Edit</b> button on any entry, and fill in the fields in your language. Your proposal arrives as
      a suggestion that we <b>review by hand</b> before publishing it: we add any missing translation,
      and stars and dates update automatically from GitHub.
    </p>

    <div class="actions" style="margin-top:18px">
      <a class="btn primary" href={localePath(lang, "contribuir")}>Contribute</a>
      <a class="btn" href={localePath(lang, "faq")}>FAQ</a>
      <a class="btn" href={GITHUB_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Repository ↗</a>
    </div>

    <p class="legal" style="margin-top:30px">
      Recompendium doesn’t host or link to ROMs or copyrighted assets. Every project requires your own
      legal copy of the game.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
